compat.c: include string.h unconditionally

memset() and strerror() are used throughout compat.c but string.h was only
pulled in inside "#ifndef HAVE_STRLCPY", so on a libc that already provides
strlcpy() (this one does) neither gets a declaration. Modern GCC treats an
implicit function declaration as a hard error, and the missing strerror()
prototype (assumed to return int) then makes syslog_perror()'s "%s" format
argument a format-string type mismatch too, which -Werror=format-security
also turns fatal.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [pptpd upstream is dead - last release 2009,
project abandoned since]

--- a/compat.c
+++ b/compat.c
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
 
 #include "compat.h"
 #include "our_syslog.h"
+#include <string.h>
 
 #ifndef HAVE_STRLCPY
 #include <string.h>
